1
00:00:20,770 --> 00:00:35,898
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you've been saying many times that the problem is that we are affected in the first place.

2
00:00:38,060 --> 00:00:42,502
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, we are... Anger is possible.

3
00:00:43,062 --> 00:00:44,443
[SPEAKER_00]: Lust is possible.

4
00:00:44,743 --> 00:00:45,664
[SPEAKER_00]: We can get confused.

5
00:00:45,944 --> 00:00:46,765
[SPEAKER_00]: Suffering is possible.

6
00:00:46,785 --> 00:00:48,586
[SPEAKER_00]: Suffering is possible for us.

7
00:00:54,131 --> 00:00:56,593
[SPEAKER_00]: Could you...

8
00:00:56,613 --> 00:01:02,858
[SPEAKER_00]: So the goal, really, is to not be affected in the first place.

9
00:01:03,759 --> 00:01:05,640
[SPEAKER_00]: Not to do something unaccounted for.

10
00:01:05,700 --> 00:01:05,940
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

11
00:01:06,981 --> 00:01:16,529
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, like, that's the kind of fundamental difference that I often try to highlight, is dhamma, the practice of dhamma,

12
00:01:17,466 --> 00:01:24,987
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not supposed to just help you with things, help you with things that bother you, help you deal with them.

13
00:01:25,007 --> 00:01:30,728
[SPEAKER_01]: It's basically supposed to uproot your liability to it, to things that bother you.

14
00:01:31,469 --> 00:01:33,149
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it doesn't manage your suffering.

15
00:01:33,589 --> 00:01:37,150
[SPEAKER_01]: It removes possibility for you to suffer in the first place.

16
00:01:40,330 --> 00:01:44,271
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you... and that's only... that is a personal goal for your practice.

17
00:01:44,451 --> 00:01:45,031
[SPEAKER_01]: It should be

18
00:01:47,415 --> 00:01:51,859
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't want to be affected by things to begin with.

19
00:01:51,879 --> 00:02:00,067
[SPEAKER_01]: Not, I want to have a perfect management technique that will always help me deal with whatever suffering have arisen.

20
00:02:00,207 --> 00:02:01,328
[SPEAKER_01]: See, that's the problem already.

21
00:02:02,029 --> 00:02:03,050
[SPEAKER_01]: Suffering has arisen.

22
00:02:03,631 --> 00:02:09,076
[SPEAKER_01]: You might be very skilled in dealing with it, but you're not skilled in not having it arise in the first place.

23
00:02:09,556 --> 00:02:10,858
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what should be your issue.

24
00:02:11,676 --> 00:02:22,526
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why, you know, the Buddha would often describe, like, his teaching, that frees you from suffering, brings cessation of being.

25
00:02:22,566 --> 00:02:30,493
[SPEAKER_01]: The ending of bhāva, the ending of these things becoming for you, manifesting, enduring, that you have to manage them later.

26
00:02:31,474 --> 00:02:32,475
[SPEAKER_00]: Means they're not anymore.

27
00:02:32,635 --> 00:02:33,516
[SPEAKER_01]: Being has ceased.

28
00:02:33,716 --> 00:02:34,217
[SPEAKER_01]: They're gone.

29
00:02:35,655 --> 00:02:37,055
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that is the goal of the dhamma.

30
00:02:37,155 --> 00:02:38,176
[SPEAKER_01]: Cessation of being.

31
00:02:38,516 --> 00:02:41,496
[SPEAKER_01]: Not management of things that are for you, here and now.

32
00:02:43,617 --> 00:02:47,098
[SPEAKER_00]: Cessation of...

33
00:02:47,118 --> 00:02:47,558
[SPEAKER_01]: Suffering.

34
00:02:48,398 --> 00:02:50,199
[SPEAKER_01]: Cessation of greed, aversion, delusion.

35
00:02:53,579 --> 00:02:56,740
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I can get angry.

36
00:02:57,760 --> 00:02:58,781
[SPEAKER_00]: I can get angry.

37
00:02:59,401 --> 00:03:03,962
[SPEAKER_00]: But then I can find out some sort of technique to manage my anger.

38
00:03:04,849 --> 00:03:07,090
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, oh, when you're angry, think these thoughts.

39
00:03:07,850 --> 00:03:08,911
[SPEAKER_01]: When you're angry, do this.

40
00:03:09,591 --> 00:03:10,511
[SPEAKER_01]: When you're angry, do that.

41
00:03:11,152 --> 00:03:11,992
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how you start.

42
00:03:12,352 --> 00:03:16,454
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to be... well, you need to want to be free from anger to begin with.

43
00:03:17,915 --> 00:03:25,318
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, inevitably, your first effort will be directed at how to manage and subdue the anger.

44
00:03:26,517 --> 00:03:33,162
[SPEAKER_01]: But you mustn't confuse that for the actual practice of the dhamma, which, if done rightly, will uproot the anger.

45
00:03:33,723 --> 00:03:35,224
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, there's nothing for you to manage.

46
00:03:35,244 --> 00:03:37,286
[SPEAKER_01]: No burden for you to have to do or deal with.

47
00:03:38,106 --> 00:03:40,408
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you don't do it, you will then suffer.

48
00:03:40,428 --> 00:03:40,548
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

49
00:03:40,588 --> 00:03:48,034
[SPEAKER_01]: There is nothing to be dealt with, because nothing affects you to begin with.

50
00:03:48,054 --> 00:03:49,415
[SPEAKER_00]: You say, it's not the dhamma.

51
00:03:49,756 --> 00:03:51,157
[SPEAKER_00]: That management is not the dhamma.

52
00:03:52,092 --> 00:03:52,412
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

53
00:03:52,452 --> 00:03:55,173
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like the approximation of the dhamma.

54
00:03:55,233 --> 00:03:57,814
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's not the direct way.

55
00:03:57,914 --> 00:04:08,999
[SPEAKER_01]: One way only, the way the Buddha would describe the dhamma, would always only result in purification of being, like removing all the unwholesomeness and making bhāva cease.

56
00:04:10,200 --> 00:04:11,300
[SPEAKER_01]: Management doesn't do that.

57
00:04:12,381 --> 00:04:20,284
[SPEAKER_01]: Only that direct insight into what uproots suffering, what is uprooting the greed, what is uprooting the aversion and delusion.

58
00:04:25,453 --> 00:04:27,554
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what is the dhamma?

59
00:04:27,694 --> 00:04:32,157
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a direct path for uprooting, not the management.

60
00:04:33,457 --> 00:04:38,380
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you are kind of responsible for whether things you're doing, whether you've taken them as dhamma,

61
00:04:39,687 --> 00:04:55,380
[SPEAKER_01]: Whether you've taken them for the purpose of uprooting their issues, not dealing with it, but, like, making yourself not liable to them, even in the future, or have you taken the dhamma instruction as a means, sort of, of helping yourself deal with these things.

62
00:04:56,501 --> 00:05:05,608
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's more like... it's determined by you, by your attitude, whether that instruction will be taken as the dhamma or as management.

63
00:05:09,729 --> 00:05:11,411
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the difference I'm pointing at.

64
00:05:12,192 --> 00:05:21,522
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, like, you can do satipaṭṭhāna, which is the dhamma, the way the Buddha described, but you can do it for the reasons of managing your suffering, not for the reasons of uprooting it.

65
00:05:24,686 --> 00:05:27,869
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means you are responsible for whether it's management or it's the dhamma.

66
00:05:28,730 --> 00:05:31,213
[SPEAKER_01]: And yeah, that's why the dhamma arises in those who see.

67
00:05:32,843 --> 00:05:34,965
[SPEAKER_01]: I can, like, you can tell the dhamma directly.

68
00:05:35,025 --> 00:05:39,569
[SPEAKER_01]: The Buddha would teach dhamma to somebody, but it wouldn't be the dhamma for them, because they're not understanding it.

69
00:05:39,729 --> 00:05:41,751
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there is no arising of dhamma in their experience.

70
00:05:42,832 --> 00:05:45,515
[SPEAKER_01]: But it is the thing the Buddha said, but they're not understanding it.

71
00:05:46,395 --> 00:05:48,677
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the dhamma can only arise on that individual level.

72
00:05:49,378 --> 00:05:52,301
[SPEAKER_00]: The dhamma, the way, knowing the knowledge of the way.

73
00:05:52,801 --> 00:05:55,343
[SPEAKER_01]: The knowledge of the way of uprooting things, basically.

74
00:05:55,363 --> 00:05:55,924
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what it is.

75
00:05:57,278 --> 00:06:05,020
[SPEAKER_01]: The knowledge of overcoming liability to suffering once and for all, not just the knowledge of how to manage it skillfully when it arises.

76
00:06:16,964 --> 00:06:22,125
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can look and see, I am subject to suffering.

77
00:06:24,514 --> 00:06:24,934
[SPEAKER_00]: I will.

78
00:06:25,835 --> 00:06:26,836
[SPEAKER_00]: Things can go wrong.

79
00:06:26,996 --> 00:06:27,837
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, exactly.

80
00:06:28,337 --> 00:06:30,019
[SPEAKER_01]: It's really on that level of I will.

81
00:06:30,679 --> 00:06:31,660
[SPEAKER_01]: I will experience this.

82
00:06:31,820 --> 00:06:32,981
[SPEAKER_01]: I am liable to this.

83
00:06:33,742 --> 00:06:34,603
[SPEAKER_01]: That's... we often say that.

84
00:06:34,623 --> 00:06:36,405
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why, like, we say the wise man will reflect.

85
00:06:36,425 --> 00:06:37,646
[SPEAKER_01]: The young man who leaves the home...

86
00:06:38,006 --> 00:06:39,327
[SPEAKER_01]: I am prey to suffering.

87
00:06:40,150 --> 00:06:44,415
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not necessarily suffering now, but the suffering will catch me at some point.

88
00:06:44,535 --> 00:06:45,356
[SPEAKER_01]: It's inevitable.

89
00:06:45,716 --> 00:06:55,907
[SPEAKER_01]: And you should start... you should have the, like, mental, basically, clarity enough, mental capacity sufficiently enough, to start seeing that as the actual root of the problem.

90
00:06:57,336 --> 00:07:00,999
[SPEAKER_01]: Not the fact that you might feel like, oh, I suffer now, I don't suffer tomorrow, or whatever.

91
00:07:01,239 --> 00:07:02,980
[SPEAKER_01]: The fact is that I will at some point.

92
00:07:03,080 --> 00:07:03,881
[SPEAKER_01]: It's inevitable.

93
00:07:04,041 --> 00:07:05,322
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the true problem.

94
00:07:06,123 --> 00:07:11,927
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if I spend the rest of my life skillfully avoiding all the major sufferings, the fact is, it will still have to...

95
00:07:12,087 --> 00:07:13,649
[SPEAKER_01]: I am still liable to it.

96
00:07:14,349 --> 00:07:16,511
[SPEAKER_01]: I still have to make the effort to avoid it.

97
00:07:16,971 --> 00:07:18,052
[SPEAKER_01]: That's already the issue.

98
00:07:18,092 --> 00:07:25,538
[SPEAKER_01]: The divine messenger, you know, the Buddha was neither sick, nor old, nor dying, when he saw a sick, aging, dead person.

99
00:07:27,545 --> 00:07:34,490
[SPEAKER_01]: So you see that, ah, this is directly present in my experience as the inherent liability.

100
00:07:35,031 --> 00:07:36,152
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's where the issue is.

101
00:07:36,892 --> 00:07:49,062
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's where the difference in practice of management and the practice of uprooting, let's call it that way, is basically in recognizing, like, okay, so there is no lust in my experience here and now.

102
00:07:49,622 --> 00:07:51,784
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no greed in my experience here and now.

103
00:07:51,844 --> 00:07:53,465
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no delusion in my experience here and now.

104
00:07:53,485 --> 00:07:54,106
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, I'm fine then.

105
00:07:54,426 --> 00:07:54,566
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

106
00:07:55,047 --> 00:07:59,050
[SPEAKER_01]: But now, as the Buddha would say, okay, so you ask yourself further.

107
00:07:59,610 --> 00:08:03,973
[SPEAKER_01]: It's good that those things are not present here and now, but your next question should now be...

108
00:08:05,134 --> 00:08:10,138
[SPEAKER_01]: So, is it possible for non-arisen lust to arise later on?

109
00:08:10,758 --> 00:08:12,840
[SPEAKER_01]: Now it's not here, but can it be here tomorrow?

110
00:08:13,140 --> 00:08:14,822
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, have I addressed that?

111
00:08:15,302 --> 00:08:15,862
[SPEAKER_01]: Here and now.

112
00:08:16,123 --> 00:08:17,864
[SPEAKER_01]: Not wait for tomorrow to address it.

113
00:08:19,045 --> 00:08:20,526
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's literally the crossroads.

114
00:08:21,579 --> 00:08:24,220
[SPEAKER_01]: Between... Are you heading in the direction of uprooting it?

115
00:08:25,121 --> 00:08:26,602
[SPEAKER_01]: Or are you heading in the direction of management?

116
00:08:27,082 --> 00:08:29,083
[SPEAKER_01]: And most people go down the path of management.

117
00:08:29,383 --> 00:08:31,164
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, I'll deal with it when it arises.

118
00:08:31,905 --> 00:08:38,228
[SPEAKER_01]: Failing to understand that liability to future lust is a risen issue now.

119
00:08:39,889 --> 00:08:41,670
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't have to wait for it to arise.

120
00:08:42,090 --> 00:08:49,154
[SPEAKER_01]: Is there a chance for non-manifested lust, non-manifested aversion to manifest in my experience later?

121
00:08:50,360 --> 00:08:52,383
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're not sure, means there is a chance.

122
00:08:53,184 --> 00:08:55,587
[SPEAKER_01]: If you know for sure, there you go, you confirmed it.

123
00:08:55,627 --> 00:08:57,770
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there is a chance that these things will arise.

124
00:08:58,150 --> 00:09:01,755
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the issue for me now, when those things are not present.

125
00:09:04,254 --> 00:09:05,615
[SPEAKER_00]: Anger is a problem.

126
00:09:05,675 --> 00:09:09,276
[SPEAKER_00]: Possibility for anger is a problem.

127
00:09:09,496 --> 00:09:10,396
[SPEAKER_00]: Now.

128
00:09:11,076 --> 00:09:11,897
[SPEAKER_00]: Not in the future.

129
00:09:12,397 --> 00:09:14,578
[SPEAKER_00]: It is now a problem.

130
00:09:14,778 --> 00:09:22,540
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what differentiates your practice from... Are you aiming at the uprooting or are you just aiming at the skillful management?

131
00:09:24,087 --> 00:09:25,748
[SPEAKER_01]: And you heard that yourself before.

132
00:09:25,808 --> 00:09:34,315
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a very common attitude of, oh, well, you know, most of the time you are not lustful, most of the time you're not angry.

133
00:09:34,375 --> 00:09:36,016
[SPEAKER_01]: So, see, you're doing fine.

134
00:09:36,376 --> 00:09:44,102
[SPEAKER_01]: If most of the time you're not angry, it means anger is still your liability, your possibility, which means you're as angry.

135
00:09:45,192 --> 00:09:50,677
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, you're not acting out of that anger as often as others, but that's just because you learned some management of your anger.

136
00:09:51,337 --> 00:09:57,843
[SPEAKER_01]: You're certainly not uprooting anything if you have the attitude that most of the time these things don't arise for me.

137
00:09:58,803 --> 00:10:01,206
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, but could they arise at any time?

138
00:10:01,566 --> 00:10:01,766
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

139
00:10:02,386 --> 00:10:05,369
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the issue you're not seeing and that's why you're not uprooting anything.

140
00:10:12,875 --> 00:10:19,281
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you see that there is a problem, because you see and know that there is the possibility of anger.

141
00:10:19,321 --> 00:10:19,501
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

142
00:10:19,541 --> 00:10:25,267
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have to train your mind to start seeing these possibilities as actually present, because they are.

143
00:10:25,567 --> 00:10:32,533
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can think to yourself, I can manage my lust or my anger quite well.

144
00:10:33,054 --> 00:10:34,075
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm really skilled at that.

145
00:10:35,080 --> 00:10:44,104
[SPEAKER_00]: I know all these different techniques, psychological methods to employ, so that I can ease that anger, ease that lust, and be calm again.

146
00:10:44,984 --> 00:10:49,966
[SPEAKER_01]: If your way out of suffering is skillful managing it, you need suffering to arise first.

147
00:10:51,687 --> 00:10:54,948
[SPEAKER_01]: So, management requires suffering in order to manage it.

148
00:10:55,248 --> 00:10:57,329
[SPEAKER_01]: So, management cannot free you from that suffering.

149
00:10:58,303 --> 00:11:00,724
[SPEAKER_01]: It can only deal with it once it has arisen.

150
00:11:01,584 --> 00:11:11,906
[SPEAKER_01]: And most people, that's the that's the kind of problem that's sort of relatively subtle, I suppose, is people, as I said, natural tendency is to turn towards management first.

151
00:11:12,386 --> 00:11:16,487
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, they can get rid of the problem, do it skillfully, whatever else.

152
00:11:16,988 --> 00:11:21,068
[SPEAKER_01]: But there is this kind of conflating that occurs then, or rather confusion.

153
00:11:21,709 --> 00:11:27,130
[SPEAKER_01]: So, through your management of suffering, you experience like less dukkha,

154
00:11:28,975 --> 00:11:32,766
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, at the same time, you kind of start...

155
00:11:33,667 --> 00:11:45,190
[SPEAKER_01]: Basically, tacitly assuming, hoping that all you have to do is manage your suffering long enough, and then somehow, magically, liability to dukkha will disappear.

156
00:11:46,711 --> 00:12:00,194
[SPEAKER_01]: So, all I have to do is adhere to this management and skillful avoiding and so on, and there's already implicit, as I said, wishful thinking that implies that you hope for suffering will then just poof, eventually.

157
00:12:00,614 --> 00:12:02,315
[SPEAKER_01]: From your management, it will just evaporate.

158
00:12:02,915 --> 00:12:03,695
[SPEAKER_01]: But it won't.

159
00:12:04,035 --> 00:12:04,656
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the point.

160
00:12:05,396 --> 00:12:08,957
[SPEAKER_01]: The only way that suffering will evaporate is if you evaporate it.

161
00:12:09,758 --> 00:12:11,098
[SPEAKER_01]: If you understand the core.

162
00:12:11,118 --> 00:12:12,459
[SPEAKER_01]: Pull the thorn out.

163
00:12:15,060 --> 00:12:16,520
[SPEAKER_01]: It won't come out by itself.

164
00:12:17,220 --> 00:12:18,301
[SPEAKER_01]: So, uproot it.

165
00:12:21,422 --> 00:12:28,725
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you keep managing it with a hope that somehow it will disappear, on the level of that I am still liable to it,

166
00:12:29,892 --> 00:12:31,973
[SPEAKER_01]: This means your practice revolves around wishful thinking.

167
00:12:32,553 --> 00:12:34,033
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no direct insight.

168
00:12:34,593 --> 00:12:37,474
[SPEAKER_01]: This is what to be done for freedom from suffering.

169
00:12:37,614 --> 00:12:42,175
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, yeah, you don't want... everyone's goal is to not have...

170
00:12:42,355 --> 00:12:42,836
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

171
00:12:43,116 --> 00:12:50,558
[SPEAKER_01]: Everybody wants to be fully free from suffering, which means everybody wants to not even be liable to discomfort and displeasure.

172
00:12:51,918 --> 00:12:55,759
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I start doing metta, metta, metta, metta, metta every day, every day, every day.

173
00:12:56,039 --> 00:12:56,900
[SPEAKER_00]: And I stay calm.

174
00:12:57,340 --> 00:12:58,080
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't get angry.

175
00:12:59,446 --> 00:13:01,407
[SPEAKER_00]: But I have to keep doing metta, because I can't.

176
00:13:01,507 --> 00:13:10,511
[SPEAKER_01]: So, your calmness, then, is dependent upon the factor of you sustaining your effort towards whatever you perceive metta contemplation to be.

177
00:13:11,251 --> 00:13:25,957
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means, if sometimes circumstances change, you get sick, or something happens, or too many things happen, you don't have enough time, so you cannot engage to that necessary extent of your metta contemplation, means you're back within anger.

178
00:13:27,017 --> 00:13:31,979
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you haven't really uprooted anger, you just suppressed it with certain practices.

179
00:13:33,320 --> 00:13:44,305
[SPEAKER_01]: And, as I said, there is not... it's not inherently wrong to do that, to begin with, but it's inherently wrong to adopt the view that doing that uproots things somehow magically.

180
00:13:45,222 --> 00:13:49,063
[SPEAKER_01]: The only way to uproot things is to directly see what uprooting is.

181
00:13:49,924 --> 00:13:51,424
[SPEAKER_01]: And I just described it, what it is.

182
00:13:51,784 --> 00:14:03,468
[SPEAKER_01]: It's recognizing that liability, the possibility of anger, possibility of ill-will, possibility of sensuality, possibility of delusion, possibility of dukkha, as the dukkha right now.

183
00:14:04,648 --> 00:14:06,649
[SPEAKER_01]: Not to wait for it when it occurs.

184
00:14:08,428 --> 00:14:10,429
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can see that possibility, see that possibility.

185
00:14:10,729 --> 00:14:11,369
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what do you do?

186
00:14:12,170 --> 00:14:16,532
[SPEAKER_00]: How do you uproot this possibility that tomorrow you might suffer?

187
00:14:16,552 --> 00:14:25,235
[SPEAKER_00]: You're being aware of that, but then... Well, it's sustaining that.

188
00:14:25,495 --> 00:14:28,757
[SPEAKER_01]: So, can you sustain that if you're not keeping the precepts?

189
00:14:28,817 --> 00:14:35,380
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you sustain that elevated state of mind that recognizes liability to dukkha without the actual dukkha being present there?

190
00:14:36,530 --> 00:14:42,115
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're drinking, smoking, running around, distracting yourself, engaging in entertainment, no, impossible.

191
00:14:43,196 --> 00:14:45,298
[SPEAKER_01]: So that the noble eightfold path, that's how you do it.

192
00:14:45,578 --> 00:14:48,901
[SPEAKER_01]: Right livelihood, right view, first, first of all.

193
00:14:48,921 --> 00:14:57,068
[SPEAKER_01]: See, if your view still regards management as the way out of suffering, you will not stay on the level of weight.

194
00:14:57,528 --> 00:15:02,593
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the possibility of suffering that's the problem that must be dealt with, not the actual suffering when it arises.

195
00:15:04,065 --> 00:15:05,567
[SPEAKER_00]: So, again, how do I deal with that?

196
00:15:06,127 --> 00:15:06,868
[SPEAKER_00]: How do you deal with what?

197
00:15:06,968 --> 00:15:07,729
[SPEAKER_00]: The possibility.

198
00:15:07,989 --> 00:15:09,130
[SPEAKER_00]: But no belief or path.

199
00:15:09,170 --> 00:15:10,372
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

200
00:15:10,392 --> 00:15:23,706
[SPEAKER_01]: So, right view, knowing that that's where the issue is, not looking elsewhere, right intentions, not entertaining, not delighting, not giving in to greed, aversion, delusion through your speech, through your body, in your thoughts.

201
00:15:25,563 --> 00:15:33,394
[SPEAKER_01]: So, on every front, you need to build yourself up in order to be able to sustain that recognition of liability here and now.

202
00:15:33,434 --> 00:15:34,375
[SPEAKER_01]: It's what the issue is.

203
00:15:34,655 --> 00:15:40,283
[SPEAKER_01]: If you ask yourself, how do I deal with the possibility of future suffering here and now?

204
00:15:40,704 --> 00:15:42,326
[SPEAKER_01]: You're basically asking, how do I manage it?

205
00:15:44,055 --> 00:15:45,175
[SPEAKER_00]: How do I manage this possibility?

206
00:15:45,195 --> 00:15:46,236
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that's what you're implying.

207
00:15:46,456 --> 00:15:48,356
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you want to deal with it now.

208
00:15:48,576 --> 00:15:56,198
[SPEAKER_01]: You deal with it properly, not... not give in to the view of management, by realizing that you have no right to resist it.

209
00:15:56,858 --> 00:16:06,441
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have to be careful if you... when you start recognizing, ah, the liability of suffering is the problem for me, you have to be careful to now not give in to the attitude of wanting to manage that.

210
00:16:08,301 --> 00:16:10,582
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I want it...

211
00:16:11,703 --> 00:16:12,143
[SPEAKER_00]: To end.

212
00:16:13,124 --> 00:16:15,246
[SPEAKER_00]: I want that possibility to be gone.

213
00:16:16,346 --> 00:16:21,330
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then you really have to understand where the dukkha is there.

214
00:16:22,311 --> 00:16:23,992
[SPEAKER_01]: Is the possibility in itself dukkha?

215
00:16:25,253 --> 00:16:32,078
[SPEAKER_01]: Is that possibility of future discomfort, future disagreeable things, is that in itself dukkha?

216
00:16:33,099 --> 00:16:33,579
[SPEAKER_00]: The idea.

217
00:16:34,360 --> 00:16:35,601
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, the thought of it.

218
00:16:35,781 --> 00:16:37,082
[SPEAKER_01]: Is that the dukkha?

219
00:16:38,979 --> 00:16:40,940
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what's the dukkha then?

220
00:16:41,260 --> 00:16:42,901
[SPEAKER_00]: I would say it's that unpleasant feeling.

221
00:16:43,802 --> 00:16:48,964
[SPEAKER_00]: It's this pleasure that is there when I have that idea.

222
00:16:50,285 --> 00:16:50,485
[SPEAKER_01]: Right.

223
00:16:51,105 --> 00:17:03,072
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I feel uncomfortable with the fact that in the future I will suffer pain which I'm suffering presently because of that.

224
00:17:03,092 --> 00:17:06,974
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then the next question you should ask yourself is the

225
00:17:08,957 --> 00:17:15,341
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the unpleasant feeling then, that you have when you think those thoughts, is that the dukkha in itself?

226
00:17:16,402 --> 00:17:17,362
No, no, no.

227
00:17:18,603 --> 00:17:21,265
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it is what it is, that pain.

228
00:17:22,145 --> 00:17:22,685
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, yeah.

229
00:17:22,725 --> 00:17:25,467
[SPEAKER_00]: The point is, I don't want it there.

230
00:17:25,707 --> 00:17:26,888
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what is then the dukkha?

231
00:17:30,370 --> 00:17:33,032
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, I would say, okay, it's the craving to get rid of it.

232
00:17:34,329 --> 00:17:34,690
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

233
00:17:34,710 --> 00:17:35,911
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how is that manifested?

234
00:17:35,931 --> 00:17:36,412
[SPEAKER_01]: What is that?

235
00:17:36,492 --> 00:17:38,936
[SPEAKER_00]: Practically, in your experience... Resistance.

236
00:17:39,376 --> 00:17:39,757
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah.

237
00:17:40,017 --> 00:17:40,498
[SPEAKER_00]: I resist that.

238
00:17:40,518 --> 00:17:42,921
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's for me that sense of entitlement of...

239
00:17:43,242 --> 00:17:44,023
[SPEAKER_01]: I should not have this.

240
00:17:44,664 --> 00:17:45,305
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you realize...

241
00:17:46,560 --> 00:17:51,464
[SPEAKER_01]: You suffer because you resist the discomfort, even before the discomfort comes your way.

242
00:17:51,965 --> 00:17:59,572
[SPEAKER_01]: See, you resist the thought of the possibility of discomfort here and now, and that's why here and now you experience dukkha.

243
00:18:00,192 --> 00:18:03,375
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the Buddha didn't say vedanā is the cause of dukkha.

244
00:18:03,495 --> 00:18:05,957
[SPEAKER_01]: He didn't say feelings are the cause of suffering.

245
00:18:06,678 --> 00:18:09,621
[SPEAKER_01]: He said taṇha, craving, is the cause of suffering.

246
00:18:10,311 --> 00:18:12,113
[SPEAKER_01]: Craving in regard to what you feel.

247
00:18:12,653 --> 00:18:24,505
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have such and such feeling when you have such and such thought, present, future or past, but it's your implicit attitude towards the feeling of either craving towards it or away from it.

248
00:18:24,705 --> 00:18:27,468
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why that experience is dukkha.

249
00:18:27,708 --> 00:18:31,712
[SPEAKER_01]: And see, you would have not seen that if you haven't forced yourself

250
00:18:32,373 --> 00:18:36,934
[SPEAKER_01]: To discern the level of that possibility of dukkha, is dukkha right now.

251
00:18:37,514 --> 00:18:47,037
[SPEAKER_01]: You wouldn't have seen that if you were just fully bent on managing the dukkha as it arises, or managing the dukkha before it arises.

252
00:18:47,157 --> 00:18:47,897
[SPEAKER_00]: Specific pain.

253
00:18:48,157 --> 00:18:48,397
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

254
00:18:48,477 --> 00:18:49,518
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what I mean.

255
00:18:49,538 --> 00:18:58,080
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, even when people recognize sometimes the future dukkha, liability to the future dukkha, they can even take that as something to manage.

256
00:18:58,100 --> 00:19:00,841
[SPEAKER_01]: Ah, so I'll do this, I'll do this, so these things will not happen to me.

257
00:19:01,921 --> 00:19:12,107
[SPEAKER_01]: Which carries the implicit view that dukkha is in those actual things, or in your actual feelings, and you don't see that it's in your attitude towards these things.

258
00:19:12,547 --> 00:19:13,648
[SPEAKER_00]: And, yeah, it's right now.

259
00:19:13,928 --> 00:19:14,649
[SPEAKER_01]: Right here, right now.

260
00:19:14,849 --> 00:19:15,749
[SPEAKER_01]: Always present.

261
00:19:15,889 --> 00:19:16,269
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

262
00:19:16,329 --> 00:19:17,850
[SPEAKER_00]: You're experiencing that pressure.

263
00:19:18,251 --> 00:19:23,694
[SPEAKER_01]: The relationship between taṇha and dukkha, between craving and suffering, it's always present.

264
00:19:24,754 --> 00:19:26,175
[SPEAKER_01]: Always simultaneously present.

265
00:19:26,535 --> 00:19:27,696
[SPEAKER_01]: Presence of suffering...

266
00:19:27,756 --> 00:19:30,718
[SPEAKER_01]: Presence of suffering means presence of taṇha.

267
00:19:31,834 --> 00:19:34,795
[SPEAKER_01]: Presence of taṇhā means presence of suffering.

268
00:19:36,356 --> 00:19:42,419
[SPEAKER_01]: So, to the extent that taṇhā is present, to that extent suffering is felt.

269
00:19:42,459 --> 00:19:46,841
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, to the extent that suffering is felt, to that extent attitude of taṇhā is present.

270
00:19:47,161 --> 00:19:52,084
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no delay, or like few seconds later dukkha comes as a result of your craving.

271
00:19:53,464 --> 00:19:53,605
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

272
00:19:53,705 --> 00:19:56,626
[SPEAKER_01]: Presence of suffering means presence of craving.

273
00:19:56,746 --> 00:19:58,547
[SPEAKER_01]: Presence of craving means presence of suffering.

274
00:19:59,695 --> 00:20:12,899
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's why if you were to remove the presence of craving, there is no suffering amidst things that used to cause you to suffer so much.

275
00:20:13,219 --> 00:20:17,240
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why liberation is visible and possible from suffering here and now.

276
00:20:17,820 --> 00:20:18,341
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's what I mean.

277
00:20:18,361 --> 00:20:26,283
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to start seeing as dukkha the liability to suffering, not actually wait for the actual suffering and then manage it.

278
00:20:30,083 --> 00:20:33,767
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you don't see that liability, then you're complacent.

279
00:20:34,287 --> 00:20:38,992
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're not seeing the liability, means you are not seeing where your craving is.

280
00:20:39,032 --> 00:20:39,872
[SPEAKER_01]: No, you're not seeing dukkha.

281
00:20:40,053 --> 00:20:40,773
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not seeing dukkha.

282
00:20:40,793 --> 00:20:40,893
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

283
00:20:41,414 --> 00:20:44,737
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to see the dukkha first, in order to see the cause of dukkha.

284
00:20:44,777 --> 00:20:48,241
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't just be dealing with the dukkha without understanding what dukkha is.

285
00:20:48,281 --> 00:20:48,861
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I said.

286
00:20:49,222 --> 00:20:51,764
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm teaching suffering and I'm teaching freedom.

287
00:20:51,784 --> 00:20:52,705
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm teaching only two things.

288
00:20:52,745 --> 00:20:53,966
[SPEAKER_01]: Suffering and freedom from it.

289
00:20:54,346 --> 00:20:54,707
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

290
00:20:56,631 --> 00:21:02,957
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you don't see that possibility of future discomfort is already dukkha now, means you're not seeing the nature of dukkha to begin with.

291
00:21:04,298 --> 00:21:09,463
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it doesn't matter what happens to you, you will not see it with the correct set of eyes, so to speak.

292
00:21:15,489 --> 00:21:21,455
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you're not seeing what the dukkha is here and now already, you're not gonna see your craving.

293
00:21:22,483 --> 00:21:26,426
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, as I said, to the extent that the dukkha is felt, to that extent craving is present.

294
00:21:26,727 --> 00:21:30,650
[SPEAKER_01]: But you're not seeing the felt dukkha, thus how can you see that which determines it?

295
00:21:30,870 --> 00:21:31,691
[SPEAKER_01]: The actual craving.

296
00:21:32,532 --> 00:21:33,092
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what I mean.

297
00:21:33,352 --> 00:21:39,438
[SPEAKER_01]: Management, looking to deal with it, over there, without seeing where the actual dukkha is,

298
00:21:40,455 --> 00:21:48,501
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you give in to that need to manage it and remove the pain, as opposed to understand it, it's it's it's inherently bound in the wrong view.

299
00:21:49,082 --> 00:21:51,183
[SPEAKER_01]: It's inherently bound in the wrong direction.

300
00:21:51,824 --> 00:21:53,245
[SPEAKER_01]: There's just like a way you could reflect.

301
00:21:53,585 --> 00:21:56,207
[SPEAKER_01]: Why are you entitled to to to manage pain?

302
00:21:57,128 --> 00:22:01,532
[SPEAKER_01]: Have you had saying it's like dwell on the nature of that dukkha?

303
00:22:02,332 --> 00:22:04,094
[SPEAKER_01]: Even the actual dukkha or the future dukkha.

304
00:22:04,114 --> 00:22:05,194
[SPEAKER_01]: Just just reflecting.

305
00:22:05,555 --> 00:22:07,016
[SPEAKER_01]: Have you had saying it's arising?

306
00:22:08,502 --> 00:22:13,484
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you prevent circumstances from becoming disagreeable at any point in the future?

307
00:22:13,524 --> 00:22:16,026
[SPEAKER_01]: Is that even conceivable, actually?

308
00:22:16,046 --> 00:22:17,186
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just a pure fantasy.

309
00:22:18,307 --> 00:22:22,369
[SPEAKER_01]: Fantasy rooted in this complete misconceiving of the problem.

310
00:22:22,509 --> 00:22:24,930
[SPEAKER_01]: Problem is in these things happening to me.

311
00:22:24,970 --> 00:22:29,812
[SPEAKER_01]: Problem is not in me implicitly resisting the idea of these things happening to me.

312
00:22:30,873 --> 00:22:33,154
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how do you stop resisting the pain?

313
00:22:35,552 --> 00:22:38,794
[SPEAKER_01]: How do you then stop craving, or rather welcoming the pleasure?

314
00:22:40,014 --> 00:22:41,215
[SPEAKER_01]: How do you remove craving?

315
00:22:41,975 --> 00:22:43,896
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, we just outlined it.

316
00:22:44,657 --> 00:22:52,261
[SPEAKER_01]: By not trying to manage it, but by seeing it here and now already.

317
00:22:53,882 --> 00:22:54,922
[SPEAKER_01]: And then by sense restraint.

318
00:22:55,142 --> 00:23:01,426
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you cannot possibly be welcoming pleasure, and then just dealing with the unpleasant experiences when they arise.

319
00:23:01,526 --> 00:23:04,167
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't just selectively pick and choose which cravings you want.

320
00:23:06,338 --> 00:23:09,999
[SPEAKER_01]: If there is any form of craving present, means liability to suffering is present.

321
00:23:11,239 --> 00:23:15,380
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have to abandon craving for pleasure, inasmuch you have to abandon craving against pain.

322
00:23:15,761 --> 00:23:17,641
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, so pain comes, pleasure comes.

323
00:23:18,381 --> 00:23:19,742
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not saying it's arising.

324
00:23:20,802 --> 00:23:29,044
[SPEAKER_01]: So, why do you then constantly entertain the attitude of, I want this, I don't want this.

325
00:23:29,484 --> 00:23:31,845
[SPEAKER_01]: I have right to want this, I have right to not want this.

326
00:23:33,743 --> 00:23:36,705
[SPEAKER_01]: Why do you go after one, try to avoid the other one?

327
00:23:36,745 --> 00:23:39,487
[SPEAKER_01]: Where is your attitude rooted?

328
00:23:39,727 --> 00:23:40,467
[SPEAKER_01]: Why are you doing it?

329
00:23:42,288 --> 00:23:46,371
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's... because pleasure and pain is... yeah, it's felt.

330
00:23:46,411 --> 00:23:47,211
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a pressure.

331
00:23:47,412 --> 00:23:49,173
[SPEAKER_00]: It's pressures you... you feel the...

332
00:23:50,028 --> 00:23:51,349
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's painful to not do it.

333
00:23:51,869 --> 00:23:52,069
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

334
00:23:52,269 --> 00:23:53,890
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you do it because you want to avoid pain.

335
00:23:54,470 --> 00:23:55,430
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's pressuring.

336
00:23:55,550 --> 00:23:56,171
[SPEAKER_00]: The pain is...

337
00:23:56,391 --> 00:23:57,151
[SPEAKER_01]: Pain is pressuring.

338
00:23:57,171 --> 00:23:57,811
[SPEAKER_01]: So, exactly.

339
00:23:57,851 --> 00:23:59,112
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you don't know how to deal with dukkha.

340
00:23:59,472 --> 00:24:00,913
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, you try to get rid of dukkha.

341
00:24:01,593 --> 00:24:05,635
[SPEAKER_01]: That's basically the nutshell of every management.

342
00:24:06,475 --> 00:24:07,976
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why I say it's inherently wrong.

343
00:24:09,236 --> 00:24:18,180
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you're doing the dhamma, all the Buddha's instruction, but you're taking them as management, means you still entertain the same attitude because of which you suffered in the first place.

344
00:24:19,030 --> 00:24:21,391
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is, I can handle this, I want to get rid of it.

345
00:24:21,871 --> 00:24:23,311
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is the attitude of craving.

346
00:24:28,253 --> 00:24:30,914
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how can you then withstand the pressure?

347
00:24:31,654 --> 00:24:33,795
[SPEAKER_00]: How can you withstand the pressure?

348
00:24:35,555 --> 00:24:36,756
[SPEAKER_00]: By withstanding the pressure.

349
00:24:36,796 --> 00:24:41,497
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, but can you withstand the pressure if even your physical behavior is not kept in check?

350
00:24:41,877 --> 00:24:43,538
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you withstand mental pressure then?

351
00:24:43,698 --> 00:24:44,218
[SPEAKER_01]: Impossible.

352
00:24:46,008 --> 00:24:57,471
[SPEAKER_01]: If your verbal behavior is not kept in check, like the outpours of lust and anger through your physical action, through your verbal action, if you don't keep a lid on that, can you possibly withstand things mentally?

353
00:24:57,591 --> 00:24:58,231
[SPEAKER_01]: Not a chance.

354
00:24:58,951 --> 00:24:59,831
[SPEAKER_01]: And how do you do that?

355
00:25:00,031 --> 00:25:00,732
[SPEAKER_01]: By doing it.

356
00:25:01,712 --> 00:25:02,932
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, how do you become stronger?

357
00:25:03,152 --> 00:25:05,933
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, by exposing yourself to things that will make you stronger.

358
00:25:06,093 --> 00:25:06,653
[SPEAKER_01]: By training.

359
00:25:08,355 --> 00:25:09,116
[SPEAKER_01]: I said that many times.

360
00:25:09,136 --> 00:25:15,780
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, even when I first read that suttas, I was a bit puzzled, but it kept talking about he trains himself thus.

361
00:25:16,540 --> 00:25:18,442
[SPEAKER_01]: A monk, skilled monk, trains himself thus.

362
00:25:19,002 --> 00:25:21,584
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, you know, you had all these notions about enlightenment and so on.

363
00:25:21,604 --> 00:25:22,965
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like, what is there to train?

364
00:25:23,025 --> 00:25:25,306
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, you do things and then enlightenment happens to you.

365
00:25:25,386 --> 00:25:25,546
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

366
00:25:26,167 --> 00:25:32,691
[SPEAKER_01]: You train the strength out of which wisdom comes, which is what enlightenment is.

367
00:25:32,711 --> 00:25:34,252
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why sensory strain is not optional.

368
00:25:35,588 --> 00:25:39,009
[SPEAKER_01]: That's where virtue is not, you know, oh, I can do it for a while.

369
00:25:39,269 --> 00:25:41,550
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a necessary basis that has to remain throughout.

370
00:25:44,391 --> 00:25:50,433
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you will be able to withstand the pressure by not giving in to pressure.

371
00:25:50,513 --> 00:25:55,414
[SPEAKER_01]: By not giving in to pressure, you get to outline what the actual problem is.

372
00:25:55,434 --> 00:26:01,436
[SPEAKER_01]: Your liability to future pressure, to future discomfort, is already a problem right here, right now.

373
00:26:03,458 --> 00:26:09,281
[SPEAKER_01]: And you see that now, because you haven't just blindly gave in to managing of the pressure in order to get rid of the pressure.

374
00:26:11,662 --> 00:26:14,043
[SPEAKER_01]: Manage the stress in order to get rid of the stress.

375
00:26:14,263 --> 00:26:18,626
[SPEAKER_01]: Practice the dhamma, so you don't get this particular stress anymore.

376
00:26:18,666 --> 00:26:24,929
[SPEAKER_00]: I recognize that there's a possibility of suffering, and I see that suffering right now.

377
00:26:25,669 --> 00:26:31,032
[SPEAKER_00]: It's suffering because I resist the displeasure here and now, in regard to the possibility.

378
00:26:34,312 --> 00:26:35,632
[SPEAKER_00]: It's suffering because I resist.

379
00:26:38,293 --> 00:26:44,634
[SPEAKER_00]: So I don't resist it, that discomfort here and now.

380
00:26:45,995 --> 00:26:46,795
[SPEAKER_00]: But I must have that...

381
00:26:47,035 --> 00:26:48,175
[SPEAKER_00]: I must still have the idea...

382
00:26:49,975 --> 00:26:54,576
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't have to keep on thinking about that there's a possibility of me suffering tomorrow.

383
00:26:55,897 --> 00:26:56,757
[SPEAKER_01]: No, that will come and go.

384
00:27:02,285 --> 00:27:05,866
[SPEAKER_00]: So, whatever... basically, I have to look at whatever I'm feeling right now.

385
00:27:05,886 --> 00:27:09,306
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, because that's where the resistance occurs.

386
00:27:09,346 --> 00:27:10,206
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what craving is.

387
00:27:10,366 --> 00:27:11,107
[SPEAKER_01]: It's where you feel.

388
00:27:11,387 --> 00:27:13,307
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, I can put the possibility of suffering...

389
00:27:13,327 --> 00:27:16,148
[SPEAKER_00]: I put that aside, because now I know what the problem is here and now.

390
00:27:16,168 --> 00:27:16,928
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure, sure.

391
00:27:17,228 --> 00:27:19,088
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I've come to that conclusion.

392
00:27:19,168 --> 00:27:24,929
[SPEAKER_01]: You contemplate the possibility of suffering when you forget what the problem is here and now, or when you're not seeing it.

393
00:27:24,949 --> 00:27:28,710
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, yeah, ask... like Sariputta used to ask himself...

394
00:27:28,950 --> 00:27:29,790
[SPEAKER_00]: I think it was Sariputta.

395
00:27:30,975 --> 00:27:31,155
[SPEAKER_00]: What?

396
00:27:31,415 --> 00:27:33,957
[SPEAKER_00]: Am I liable to anything, to any suffering?

397
00:27:34,037 --> 00:27:34,857
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it something like that?

398
00:27:34,917 --> 00:27:35,658
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, it was something like that.

399
00:27:35,678 --> 00:27:37,018
[SPEAKER_00]: Is there any suffering that can arise in me?

400
00:27:37,038 --> 00:27:43,302
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, is there any suffering that hasn't... that the possibility of suffering hasn't been, like, addressed, so to speak?

401
00:27:43,422 --> 00:27:43,622
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

402
00:27:43,642 --> 00:27:46,223
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's like, no, I don't see any.

403
00:27:46,644 --> 00:27:46,904
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

404
00:27:47,622 --> 00:27:49,463
[SPEAKER_01]: He still doesn't decide, okay, I'm done now.

405
00:27:50,003 --> 00:27:50,143
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

406
00:27:50,243 --> 00:27:52,785
[SPEAKER_01]: You remain with that vigilant attitude.

407
00:27:53,305 --> 00:27:54,826
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't say, okay, I'll check.

408
00:27:55,006 --> 00:27:57,368
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll check in every now and then and see if something has changed.

409
00:27:57,848 --> 00:28:05,352
[SPEAKER_01]: The possibility for lust, aversion, delusion, possibility for pressure, possibility for central pull, possibility for suffering.

410
00:28:06,192 --> 00:28:07,013
[SPEAKER_01]: Has that arisen now?

411
00:28:07,953 --> 00:28:13,016
[SPEAKER_01]: Not the actual suffering, but the possibility for it being, oh, look, yeah, now I see it.

412
00:28:13,096 --> 00:28:14,097
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, it could, it's possible.

413
00:28:14,817 --> 00:28:15,198
[SPEAKER_01]: There you go.

414
00:28:15,338 --> 00:28:15,879
[SPEAKER_01]: That's your issue.

415
00:28:16,179 --> 00:28:16,720
[SPEAKER_01]: Right there and then.

416
00:28:17,621 --> 00:28:18,262
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what do you do?

417
00:28:19,464 --> 00:28:21,607
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you don't lose the sight of it, to begin with.

418
00:28:21,748 --> 00:28:22,329
[SPEAKER_00]: That feeling.

419
00:28:22,529 --> 00:28:22,749
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

420
00:28:22,990 --> 00:28:29,820
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't welcome it, if it's the possibility towards pleasure and sensuality, and you don't resist it, if it's the possibility towards pain and discomfort.

421
00:28:33,344 --> 00:28:39,650
[SPEAKER_00]: Is there anything that... is there a possibility for me... indulging in... Welcoming.

422
00:28:39,910 --> 00:28:41,532
[SPEAKER_01]: Indulging is too coarse already.

423
00:28:41,572 --> 00:28:43,354
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the result of pleasure in the future.

424
00:28:43,594 --> 00:28:43,914
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

425
00:28:43,954 --> 00:28:51,781
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's on the level of the possibility of pleasure or possibility of pain, which means on the level of welcoming or kind of shying away from.

426
00:28:52,022 --> 00:28:52,622
[SPEAKER_01]: It's subtler.

427
00:28:53,183 --> 00:28:58,528
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I think, if someone brings me a pizza tomorrow, I love the taste of pizza, will I

428
00:28:59,610 --> 00:29:01,930
[SPEAKER_00]: Will I welcome that pleasurable taste?

429
00:29:01,950 --> 00:29:02,811
[SPEAKER_00]: Will I get lost?

430
00:29:03,071 --> 00:29:07,431
[SPEAKER_01]: And you will know that, if you're looking at things rightly, you will know that already now.

431
00:29:08,152 --> 00:29:08,512
[SPEAKER_01]: You'll know.

432
00:29:08,692 --> 00:29:09,752
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, I can't wait.

433
00:29:10,112 --> 00:29:19,394
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you are perfectly restrained and don't express your joy over the particular type of food brought to you, you already, internally, you know what it is.

434
00:29:19,654 --> 00:29:22,734
[SPEAKER_00]: And so that... And that's the problem.

435
00:29:22,754 --> 00:29:23,414
[SPEAKER_00]: But there's the work.

436
00:29:24,055 --> 00:29:25,095
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the work, exactly.

437
00:29:25,115 --> 00:29:25,935
[SPEAKER_01]: That's where the issue is.

438
00:29:28,085 --> 00:29:32,928
[SPEAKER_01]: And people would... okay, so I will tell them never to bring that thing again, because I like it too much.

439
00:29:34,129 --> 00:29:39,412
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like, well, you can do that as a way of, like, if you really can't deal with things, so, okay, put it aside.

440
00:29:40,013 --> 00:29:41,974
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's really masking the issue.

441
00:29:43,035 --> 00:29:46,036
[SPEAKER_01]: The issue is not the pizza or whatever people have brought to you.

442
00:29:46,597 --> 00:29:48,718
[SPEAKER_01]: The issue is you're unable to restrain yourself.

443
00:29:49,379 --> 00:29:51,920
[SPEAKER_01]: You're unable to have mind free from greed.

444
00:29:52,701 --> 00:29:55,222
[SPEAKER_01]: So now you're just trying to remove things that make you greedy.

445
00:29:55,342 --> 00:29:57,824
[SPEAKER_00]: But... You're unable to not be...

446
00:29:59,192 --> 00:29:59,992
[SPEAKER_00]: Wanting pleasure.

447
00:30:00,253 --> 00:30:04,554
[SPEAKER_01]: You are unable to, exactly, to not welcome that pressure of that pleasure.

448
00:30:04,774 --> 00:30:05,315
[SPEAKER_01]: You're unable.

449
00:30:05,455 --> 00:30:07,316
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means, that's why you will remain liable.

450
00:30:08,036 --> 00:30:11,698
[SPEAKER_01]: You can spend hundred years of that management, you will never uproot your sensuality.

451
00:30:12,498 --> 00:30:18,360
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you feel the pressure of pain, that resistance against it, and you stop.

452
00:30:19,521 --> 00:30:20,021
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, exactly.

453
00:30:20,381 --> 00:30:21,602
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't try to get rid of it.

454
00:30:23,128 --> 00:30:27,469
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't try to psychologize, manage, explain, prevent things from arising.

455
00:30:27,769 --> 00:30:39,133
[SPEAKER_01]: Obviously, if something obviously unwholesome, like... like when that man came and offered... wanted to offer his daughter to the Buddha, and we say, no, don't even bother, I will not accept that.

456
00:30:40,453 --> 00:30:44,374
[SPEAKER_01]: Like certain things cannot be done rightly, such as sexual intercourse and so on.

457
00:30:45,275 --> 00:30:49,156
[SPEAKER_01]: But there are things that there's nothing... like there's nothing wrong in eating food, whether it's tasty or not.

458
00:30:51,154 --> 00:30:53,275
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's wrong in eating it with sensual reasons.

459
00:30:53,916 --> 00:31:09,484
[SPEAKER_01]: And you eat... And you eat the... you eat your food with sensual reasons, when... when you are unable to... to not be overwhelmed by that pressure towards accepting pleasure.

460
00:31:11,725 --> 00:31:16,888
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why the Buddha would say, like, when you... when you practice in regard to the food, not like you stop eating,

461
00:31:17,955 --> 00:31:18,155
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

462
00:31:18,515 --> 00:31:19,436
[SPEAKER_01]: You eat mindfully.

463
00:31:20,016 --> 00:31:24,058
[SPEAKER_01]: You maintain the reasons for your eating clearly, and you eat while those reasons are present.

464
00:31:24,178 --> 00:31:28,161
[SPEAKER_01]: Reasons for health, sustaining the body, so that you can practice further.

465
00:31:28,221 --> 00:31:31,742
[SPEAKER_01]: Not reasons of beautifications, chasing the taste, and so on.

466
00:31:32,723 --> 00:31:38,586
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you just think, if I avoid the food, I don't have to deal with these reasons, well, you're sorely mistaken.

467
00:31:39,507 --> 00:31:41,768
[SPEAKER_01]: You're just covering up the basis of your centrality.

468
00:31:43,770 --> 00:31:54,903
[SPEAKER_01]: So, even if you just eat horrible stuff all the time, because you don't want to experience any sensual joy of the taste, you still need to look at these reasons and keep the vigilant eye on them, because that's where sensuality is rooted.

469
00:31:56,204 --> 00:31:57,586
[SPEAKER_01]: Not in the actual taste.

470
00:32:01,745 --> 00:32:02,765
[SPEAKER_01]: So, am I welcoming?

471
00:32:02,885 --> 00:32:03,685
[SPEAKER_01]: Am I delighting?

472
00:32:03,825 --> 00:32:10,247
[SPEAKER_01]: Am I accepting this elation on account of central prospect that is pleasing to me?

473
00:32:10,307 --> 00:32:11,147
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the issue.

474
00:32:11,247 --> 00:32:12,047
[SPEAKER_01]: My acceptance.

475
00:32:12,127 --> 00:32:13,467
[SPEAKER_01]: Not seeing the danger in it.

476
00:32:14,327 --> 00:32:16,448
[SPEAKER_01]: Not... That's the issue.

477
00:32:16,508 --> 00:32:21,469
[SPEAKER_01]: Not the issue is, oh, I must not have this thing arise for me at all.

478
00:32:23,469 --> 00:32:29,430
[SPEAKER_01]: Call people, tell them never to bring their food, never to... you'll never see anything, you'll never hear anything, you'll never taste anything agreeable.

479
00:32:30,962 --> 00:32:33,703
[SPEAKER_01]: And you're not... you're misconceiving where the issue is.

480
00:32:33,983 --> 00:32:34,203
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

481
00:32:34,904 --> 00:32:36,124
[SPEAKER_00]: And you're maintaining the issue.

482
00:32:36,885 --> 00:32:38,645
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you remain fully within it, yeah.

483
00:32:38,725 --> 00:32:41,447
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why you're not... you're not stepping outside of central domain.

484
00:32:41,527 --> 00:32:55,193
[SPEAKER_01]: There might be someone that comes along... And that's why the asceticism, you know, the atma's form, like, you know, in giants, you know, the perfect practice of obsessive ahimsa, non-violence, not eating, not harming anything, it's utterly misguided.

485
00:32:55,833 --> 00:32:57,173
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's fully misprojected.

486
00:32:58,454 --> 00:33:00,195
[SPEAKER_01]: They don't see where the issue really is.

487
00:33:01,142 --> 00:33:10,949
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, avoiding food, any food, just eating every third day or something, to the point of, in the end, you die on account of starvation, and that's like the ultimate goal of a Jain.

488
00:33:11,489 --> 00:33:16,753
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, you realize you fully remain bound by the central domain, never a step closer to escape it.

489
00:33:17,534 --> 00:33:29,322
[SPEAKER_00]: It was the Jains that said that by avoiding things, then you diminish the pressure, but...

490
00:33:31,284 --> 00:33:32,385
[SPEAKER_00]: Where's the Buddhist ways?

491
00:33:33,506 --> 00:33:33,726
[SPEAKER_01]: What?

492
00:33:33,826 --> 00:33:35,407
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's not always the same things.

493
00:33:35,868 --> 00:33:37,989
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't always have one attitude towards all things.

494
00:33:38,830 --> 00:33:46,056
[SPEAKER_01]: If you were to only have the attitude of accepting everything, every type of feeling, every type of perception, then Jains would have been right.

495
00:33:46,676 --> 00:33:48,538
[SPEAKER_01]: All you need to do is avoid it, because you always accept it.

496
00:33:49,899 --> 00:33:50,760
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's not the case.

497
00:33:51,660 --> 00:33:54,643
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not accepting the unpleasurable feelings.

498
00:33:55,463 --> 00:33:57,085
[SPEAKER_01]: You are actually trying to get rid of them.

499
00:33:58,046 --> 00:33:59,587
[SPEAKER_01]: You're only accepting certain things.

500
00:34:00,289 --> 00:34:03,492
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, you're resisting some other things.

501
00:34:03,552 --> 00:34:04,993
[SPEAKER_01]: And so, it's not a blanket rule.

502
00:34:05,633 --> 00:34:08,416
[SPEAKER_01]: Always avoid, because it's not a blanket experience.

503
00:34:09,176 --> 00:34:09,977
[SPEAKER_01]: That's always the same.

504
00:34:11,518 --> 00:34:18,824
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why, what you do, whether you avoid, or whether you have to basically endure, is determined by how you feel.

505
00:34:19,105 --> 00:34:22,167
[SPEAKER_01]: And you don't even feel that it's always the same in regard to the same things.

506
00:34:23,068 --> 00:34:25,150
[SPEAKER_01]: Sometimes, one same food might make you happy.

507
00:34:25,210 --> 00:34:26,651
[SPEAKER_01]: Sometimes, it might make you unpleasant.

508
00:34:27,371 --> 00:34:29,073
[SPEAKER_01]: Sometimes, it might make you indifferent.

509
00:34:30,512 --> 00:34:36,994
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means you have three different types of feelings, you have three different types of attitude, that will then exhibit the craving.

510
00:34:37,695 --> 00:34:45,738
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you always avoid everything, you're ignoring two-thirds of that picture, and that's why you will remain ignorant.

511
00:34:48,259 --> 00:34:51,820
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how can you then know when to reject and when to accept?

512
00:34:52,881 --> 00:34:56,502
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, when you're resisting gratuitously the future prospect of suffering,

513
00:34:58,730 --> 00:35:00,193
[SPEAKER_01]: Should you be rejecting that even more?

514
00:35:00,514 --> 00:35:00,654
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

515
00:35:01,035 --> 00:35:04,802
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to accept that these possibilities are there, present, and you have no say in it.

516
00:35:05,543 --> 00:35:10,032
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how you can then stop resisting it gratuitously, and thus you approve the suffering.

517
00:35:14,386 --> 00:35:18,910
[SPEAKER_01]: Or should you then accept the prospect of future pleasure that you are already delighting in?

518
00:35:19,070 --> 00:35:19,211
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

519
00:35:19,331 --> 00:35:20,091
[SPEAKER_01]: You must reject it.

520
00:35:20,131 --> 00:35:21,673
[SPEAKER_01]: You must contemplate the danger in it.

521
00:35:22,374 --> 00:35:23,515
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how do you know what you do?

522
00:35:24,135 --> 00:35:27,398
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, by learning how to be properly mindful of an enduring feeling.

523
00:35:27,819 --> 00:35:29,140
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's where things converge.

524
00:35:29,220 --> 00:35:30,161
[SPEAKER_01]: That's where things are rooted.

525
00:35:32,003 --> 00:35:36,927
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't just take a set of behavior that you always do this when it's this and this when it's that.

526
00:35:37,688 --> 00:35:37,828
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

527
00:35:39,051 --> 00:35:46,136
[SPEAKER_01]: First, you just need to remain mindful of what's the present enduring feeling, and you will know then, what attitude is there.

528
00:35:46,877 --> 00:35:53,341
[SPEAKER_01]: If the present enduring feeling is of pleasure and agreeability, means you have the attitude of welcoming it, by default.

529
00:35:53,722 --> 00:35:54,302
[SPEAKER_00]: But you must...

530
00:35:54,802 --> 00:35:56,404
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's... that's what you reject.

531
00:35:56,804 --> 00:35:59,606
[SPEAKER_01]: You reject your welcoming of this central pleasure.

532
00:36:00,647 --> 00:36:04,209
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't blame, like giants, the actual object of your central pleasure.

533
00:36:06,090 --> 00:36:08,072
[SPEAKER_01]: So you will know what attitude is there,

534
00:36:09,552 --> 00:36:12,253
[SPEAKER_01]: By correctly understanding what present feeling is there.

535
00:36:14,394 --> 00:36:16,515
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the root of the problem.

536
00:36:16,635 --> 00:36:17,515
[SPEAKER_01]: It's always present.

537
00:36:17,755 --> 00:36:19,196
[SPEAKER_01]: The root of the problem is always present.

538
00:36:19,256 --> 00:36:20,036
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not in the past.

539
00:36:20,096 --> 00:36:20,836
[SPEAKER_01]: It will not be in the future.

540
00:36:20,856 --> 00:36:22,097
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the root of the problem.

541
00:36:22,297 --> 00:36:22,817
[SPEAKER_01]: Attitude.

542
00:36:22,917 --> 00:36:23,337
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

543
00:36:23,617 --> 00:36:28,159
[SPEAKER_01]: And the root, that present root, is basically your attitude towards the presently enduring feeling.

544
00:36:28,947 --> 00:36:34,149
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's whatever suffering will arise in the future, it's always... That's the gateway.

545
00:36:34,189 --> 00:36:34,749
[SPEAKER_01]: It's within that.

546
00:36:34,829 --> 00:36:36,790
[SPEAKER_01]: That doesn't disappear when the suffering arrives.

547
00:36:36,810 --> 00:36:40,791
[SPEAKER_01]: That remains its vessel, so to speak, its framework, as I say.

548
00:36:44,033 --> 00:36:50,915
[SPEAKER_01]: Because no amount of future discomfort and suffering can affect you, unless you're holding on to that vessel of rejecting it.

549
00:36:51,575 --> 00:36:53,956
[SPEAKER_01]: Like the vessel of rejection, basically, of any discomfort.

550
00:36:54,397 --> 00:36:55,297
[SPEAKER_01]: That's your mindset.

551
00:36:56,383 --> 00:37:01,065
[SPEAKER_01]: So, anything that comes of discomfortable nature, it's going to make you suffer because of your attitude of not wanting it.

552
00:37:02,266 --> 00:37:14,491
[SPEAKER_01]: You remove the attitude of that gratuitous attitude of not wanting the discomfort, not necessarily seeking it out, but certainly not trying to get rid of it, when it when it's there, exactly, when it's circumstantial arise, which inevitably will.

553
00:37:15,311 --> 00:37:22,834
[SPEAKER_01]: Ah, then you're not cultivating that gratuitous mindset of resistance, which means your craving is now truly diminishing.

554
00:37:24,015 --> 00:37:24,335
[SPEAKER_01]: And same,

555
00:37:26,032 --> 00:37:35,039
[SPEAKER_01]: You will diminish your craving when you don't cultivate Gacchuri's mindset of welcoming, as the Buddha would say, delighting, entertaining the notions of current and future pleasure.

556
00:37:36,160 --> 00:37:40,444
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, why you... like, when you ask yourself, am I liable to future essential desire?

557
00:37:41,004 --> 00:37:41,284
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yes.

558
00:37:41,625 --> 00:37:41,905
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

559
00:37:42,205 --> 00:37:45,348
[SPEAKER_01]: Because here and now, I'm still holding it dear.

560
00:37:45,628 --> 00:37:47,529
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm still welcoming the prospect of it.

561
00:37:48,070 --> 00:37:49,931
[SPEAKER_01]: Here and now, I haven't uprooted it.

562
00:37:50,031 --> 00:37:54,315
[SPEAKER_01]: Not, I haven't dealt with it rightly once it has arisen.

563
00:37:55,094 --> 00:37:55,274
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

564
00:37:55,994 --> 00:38:05,256
[SPEAKER_01]: It's on the level of... that possible future sensuality is only possible because, right here, right now, I am still valuing it.

565
00:38:05,956 --> 00:38:08,757
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm still... haven't seen the thorough danger in it.

566
00:38:08,877 --> 00:38:11,418
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm still, on some level, delighting in it.

567
00:38:11,698 --> 00:38:17,319
[SPEAKER_01]: And that delight is to be dealt with, here and now, not in the future when that thing arises.

